Use `Export records` to save the active project's records as a CSV, TSV, Excel,
or JSON file. A saved record-export preset defines which records become rows,
which columns are included, and how headers are named.

## Quick way to export records

{% steps %}

1. Open the project `Dashboard` menu and select `Export records`.
2. Choose an `Export preset` {% ref id="A" /%}. To create or edit presets,
   select `Manage presets` {% ref id="B" /%}.
3. Review the preset summary (record type, header format, and columns) and
   select `Preview Table` {% ref id="D" /%} to inspect real rows. On a phone,
   open the `Preview` tab {% ref id="F" /%}.
4. Choose a file format {% ref id="C" /%} and enter a file name. Turn on
   `Append date` {% ref id="G" /%} to add today's date to the name. JSON cannot
   use Darwin Core headers.
5. Choose a destination folder on desktop and select `Export records` {% ref id="E" /%}.
6. Inspect the exported file for rows, headers, units, and repeated values. Use
   `Share`, open the folder, save a copy, or select `Export another`.

{% /steps %}

## Deep dive {% #deep-dive %}

A record-export preset is a reusable recipe for one table. It defines three
things:

- **Rows:** the `Record type`, and for specimens the `Specimen taxon group`
  (`All taxa`, `Mammals`—which includes bats, `Birds`, `Herpetofauna`,
  `Invertebrates`, or `Fishes`).
- **Columns:** the fields configured under `Edit Fields`.
- **Headers:** set by `Generated header format` or overridden per column.

Edit presets under `Settings` > `Tabular Data`, not on the export screen. Field
and format edits save automatically; the preset's name and optional
`Description` (up to 80 characters, shown under the name in the list) save when
you select `Update`. Use `Duplicate` to build a new preset from an existing
one.

### What one row is

| Record type | One row per |
| --- | --- |
| `Specimen` | Specimen. Parts and other repeated details can be joined into one cell or spread across numbered columns. |
| `Specimen parts` | Specimen part (such as a skin or tissue tube), with its specimen details repeated on each row. Ideal for tissue inventories. |
| `Site` | Site. |
| `Collecting event` | Collecting event. |
| `Narrative` | Narrative entry. |
| `Symbionts` | Symbiont record. |

### Kinds of columns

Each column in `Selected Mappings` uses one of four `Mapping type` options:

| Mapping type | Use it for | Example output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Single field` | One NAHPU field, stored as-is or reformatted. | `Rattus` |
| `List field` | Multiple fields joined together, skipping empty values. | `Indonesia, West Java, Cibodas` |
| `Custom field` | Fields mixed with fixed text, or fixed text alone. | `HH-156` |
| `Nested records` | Repeated child records, such as specimen parts or collectors. | `Skin; Skull; Tissue` |

Check a field under `Insert Source Field` (`Available Fields` on phones) to add
it as a `Single field`. Use `Add custom field` and `Add nested` for the other
types. Select a column's edit icon to configure it, and drag its handle to
reorder. See [Export expressions](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#custom-fields)
for custom fields and conditional output.

### Format a single field

A `Single field` column can transform its value using `Value format`:

| Value format | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `Normal text` | Writes the value as stored. |
| `Encoded text` | Writes the human-readable label for a stored code, such as `Male` for `0`. |
| `Coordinates` | Formats coordinates using the style under `Advanced`. |
| `Date` | Formats dates using `Advanced` > `Raw format option`, such as `dd/mm/yyyy`. |
| `Conditional brackets`, `Conditional field`, `Conditional value` | Brackets or replaces the value when conditions match; see [Conditional output](/en/usages/export/export-expressions/#conditional-output). |

Every column type also supports `Replace text` to rewrite formatted values—for
example, renaming a part type to match a museum database.

### Repeated values

When a specimen has multiple parts, collectors, or collecting methods,
`List field` and `Nested records` columns control how those values appear:

| Output | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `One column` | All values in one cell, joined by your chosen separator. |
| `Indexed columns` | One column (or group of columns) per value, such as `prep_1_type`, `prep_2_type`, … The count matches the record with the most values. |
| `Repeated rows` | Nested records only: one row per value, repeating the parent record. At most one column per preset can use this. |

With `Indexed columns` or `Repeated rows`, each child field of a nested column
can take its own `Column name (optional)`. Place `{n}` where the index belongs:
`PrepType{n}Count` writes `PrepType1Count`, `PrepType2Count`, and so on. A name
without `{n}` uses the `Indexed column names` numbering style.

Preview a record with multiple values to confirm the layout suits the receiving
tool.

### Column headers

`Generated header format` names any column without an explicit `Column name`:

| Format | Header for `specimen::uuid` |
| --- | --- |
| `table::fieldName` | `specimen::uuid` |
| `fieldName` | `uuid` |
| `Darwin Core (dwc:/dcterms:)` | `dwc:occurrenceID` |
| `NAHPU namespace (nahpu:table.field)` | `nahpu:specimen.uuid` |

An explicit `Column name` always overrides the generated header.

With Darwin Core headers, fields without a Darwin Core term keep a NAHPU
header rather than forcing an approximate term. Darwin Core output also:

- credits the identification to the specimen's determiner;
- preserves verbatim coordinates;
- adds a positive specimen coordinate extent to a positive coordinate
  uncertainty—verify this combined value against your project's
  [georeferencing method](/en/best-practices/data-collection/#georeferencing).

Measurement units are not converted; verify them in the exported table.

### Blank columns

If a receiving spreadsheet expects a column NAHPU does not record (such as
continent), add a custom field with that `Column name` and leave its expression
empty. NAHPU writes an empty column so your columns line up.

### Additional observation fields

Custom fields defined in `Settings` (such as environmental observations) export
like built-in fields, using keys like `customEnvironment::<definition UUID>`.
Event and specimen exports include stored values even if those fields are
currently hidden on forms; hiding a field never filters it out of exports.

### File formats

| Format | Best for |
| --- | --- |
| `CSV` or `TSV` | Exchange with databases, R, Python, and other scripts. |
| `Excel` | Reviewing tables in a spreadsheet app. |
| `JSON` | Software consuming JSON directly (Darwin Core headers are unavailable). |

Always inspect the exported file in the destination tool—spreadsheets can strip
leading zeros from IDs or reformat dates automatically.

The [LSUMZ Mammalogy tutorial](/en/tutorials/export/specify-export-preset/)
walks through building a complete museum database preset using most of these
options.

## Transfers and recovery

A record export is one-way: NAHPU cannot import it back as a project. To move
or restore data in NAHPU, use:

- a [project transfer](/en/usages/export/project-transfer/) or
  [NAHPU Data Package](/en/usages/export/export-bundles/) with
  `Import project` on the home screen or `Merge project` inside an open
  project;
- a [database backup](/en/usages/export/#backup-database) to restore the entire
  installation.

To share a single preset with your team, see
[Create a tabular export preset](/en/how-to-recipes/export-and-print/create-export-preset/).
